# claude-memory-kit knobs. Copied to ~/.claude/memory-kit/config on install if no
# config exists yet; upgrades never overwrite it. Uncomment a line to change a value.
#
# Rules: KEY=value, one per line. Surrounding spaces are trimmed. A malformed value
# is ignored and the built-in default applies. An environment variable with the same
# name overrides this file. This file is read, never executed, so nothing here can
# run code.
#
# This is also the inventory: every knob the kit reads is listed below. Anything
# else that looks like a setting in the source is internal.

# Skip the daily feedback miner on this machine. Memory, the index, and the
# guardrail carry on. Setting it also stops the health notice treating the miner's
# silence as a fault. Off values: 0, no, off, false, or empty.
#MEMORY_KIT_NO_MINER=1

# Days a feature must stay blocked before the health notice mentions it. Raise it
# on a machine that is offline a lot.
#MEMORY_KIT_HEALTH_GRACE=3

# Model the daily miner runs on.
#MEMORY_KIT_MINER_MODEL=sonnet

# Seconds between "memory files changed" notices in a running session.
#MEMORY_KIT_DELTA_THROTTLE=3600

# What this machine calls itself in review-marker commits. Defaults to the short
# hostname. Set it when the hostname is not the name you think of the machine by.
#MEMORY_KIT_MACHINE_LABEL=laptop

# Who makes the change: managed means the kit does it and says what it will do
# first; advisory means the kit reports and you make the change yourself. Neither
# is ever fully automatic over your own memory files.
#
# You do not normally edit this. install.sh requires --mode=managed|advisory on a
# first install and writes the answer here, so later upgrades need no flag. Passing
# --mode again overrides it and says the mode changed.
# See docs/DESIGN-memory.md D8 and docs/DESIGN-install.md D11.
#MEMORY_KIT_MODE=advisory
